= Machine consciousness =

== Definition ==
'''Machine consciousness''' concerns whether artificial systems could possess consciousness, what the relevant criteria would be, and how such claims might be assessed. It is a research question at the intersection of [[Artificial intelligence]], [[Cognition]], [[Consciousness]], [[Computer science]], and philosophy of mind, not a synonym for machine intelligence or [[AI agent|agency]].

== Functions versus experience ==
An agent can sense, plan, learn, remember, maintain [[Agent self-identity|operational identity]], report on its own computations, and make decisions. These observable or engineered functions do not, by themselves, settle whether the system has phenomenal experience. Distinguish '''behavioral performance''', '''internal architecture''', '''introspective claims''', and '''subjective experience''' as separate evidential questions.

== Theories and indicators ==
Computational and neuroscientific theories suggest potentially relevant properties, such as recurrent processing, global accessibility of information, higher-order monitoring, attention models and feedback-based integration. The Butlin and colleagues (2023) interdisciplinary report proposed using theory-derived indicator properties to evaluate AI architectures. A later peer-reviewed methodological article (2025) also advocated indicators and calibrated uncertainty. An indicator is not proof: theories differ, implementation details matter, and there is no accepted universal diagnostic.

== Substrate and embodiment ==
Physical implementation matters to some theories and may not play the same role in others. Claims of absolute substrate independence, or absolute impossibility for machines, require arguments beyond one computational analogy. Biological neurons and silicon circuits implement information-processing operations through different physical mechanisms. Engineering similarity does not establish phenomenological equivalence.

== Assessment and uncertainty ==
A meaningful research program specifies a theory, measurable candidate mechanisms, confounds, alternative hypotheses and the uncertainty of inferences. Reports of first-person experience from language models may reflect learned linguistic and training behavior; the mere presence or absence of such reports does not decide the issue.

== Relationship to BITwiki systems ==
[[State continuity]] and [[Informational inheritance]] concern persistence and derivation of information or state. They do not entail [[Consciousness]]. [[Metacognition]] and [[Self-modeling agent]] concern computational descriptions and abilities that may figure in some theories of consciousness without constituting universal tests.

